// ============================================================================
// palindrome-check.epher -- numbers that read the same both ways
//
// A number is a palindrome when it equals its own reversal. The script
// reverses with a recursive def (one level per digit) and compares, then
// counts the palindromes below 1000 with a packed while loop (guide
// 1.30). The count is the classic one: 9 one-digit palindromes (1 to 9),
// 9 two-digit ones (11, 22, ..., 99) and 90 three-digit ones (aba with a
// from 1 to 9 and b from 0 to 9) total 108.
//
// Source: standard recreational number theory; see David Wells, "The
// Penguin Dictionary of Curious and Interesting Numbers". All results
// are exact.
//
// Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/palindrome-check.epher"
// In the REPL:             load epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/palindrome-check.epher
// Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
//
// There is no knob: the sample numbers and bound 1000 are the checks.
// ============================================================================

// ---- the reversal helper ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// dlen(n) counts digits; rev(n) puts the last digit in front: the digit
// moves to the 10^(digits - 1) place, then the rest reverses after it.
def dlen(n) = if n < 10 then 1 else 1 + dlen(floor(n / 10))
def rev(n) = if n < 10 then n else mod(n, 10) * 10 ^ (dlen(n) - 1) + rev(floor(n / 10))

// ---- named checks --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
print("rev(12321):", rev(12321))                    // the same number
print("12321 is a palindrome:", rev(12321) == 12321)
print("rev(12345):", rev(12345))                    // 54321, not the same
print("12345 is a palindrome:", rev(12345) == 12345)
print("1001 is a palindrome:", rev(1001) == 1001)   // the smallest four-digit one

// ---- count the palindromes below 1000 ---------------------------------------------------------------
// v = {candidate, count}; every value equal to its reversal scores.
v = {1, 0}
while v[1] < 1000 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if rev(v[1]) == v[1] then 1 else 0)}
print("palindromes below 1000:", v[2])              // 9 + 9 + 90 = 108
print("count by shape:", 9 + 9 + 90)
// ---- expected output ----
// = rev(12321): 12321
// = 12321 is a palindrome: true
// = rev(12345): 54321
// = 12345 is a palindrome: false
// = 1001 is a palindrome: true
// = {1, 0}
// = palindromes below 1000: 108
// = count by shape: 108
